course: dictionary
lesson: 1171
title: Broadcaster
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A broadcaster is a company or a person that sends out news and shows on the radio or on TV. The BBC is a broadcaster. So is the ABC, the CBC, RTÉ and PBS. A broadcaster can also be a person, such as a presenter who speaks on TV or the radio to share the news.
explain: Read the word in the sentence: «public broadcasters such as the BBC, ABC or CBC are paid for by the public and meant to report freely.» The names after «such as» are examples, so they show you what a broadcaster is. The words «paid for by the public» tell you who owns these broadcasters: ordinary people, not the government.
explain: Notice the word «report» in the sentence. A broadcaster should tell the news fairly and honestly. The sentence also says that when a government controls the TV, it can show only good news about its leader. A broadcaster that does this is not reporting freely.
fact: A broadcaster sends out radio or TV programmes to many people at once.
fact: The word «broadcaster» can mean a company, like the BBC, or a person who presents programmes on radio or TV.
fact: The BBC, ABC, CBC, RTÉ and PBS are all broadcasters.
fact: Sir David Attenborough is a famous broadcaster who shares programmes about nature.
exercise: What does a broadcaster do? | Builds TV sets in a factory | *Sends out radio or TV programmes | Sells newspapers from door to door | Writes school textbooks
exercise: Which of these is named as a broadcaster in the sentence «public broadcasters such as the BBC, ABC or CBC are paid for by the public»? | The government | Sky | *ABC | The leader
exercise: «Sir David Attenborough is a famous broadcaster.» What does this tell you? | *He is a person who presents programmes on TV | He is a company that makes radios | He is a newspaper reporter who writes only about sport | He is a government leader
test: «Broadcasters should report fairly.» What should a broadcaster do? | *Report the news fairly | Keep the news secret | Make up stories to entertain | Show only good news about the leader
test: The BBC, CBC, ABC, RTÉ and PBS are examples of what? | Newspapers | Governments | *Broadcasters | People who present the news
exam: Why are public broadcasters meant to report freely? | To sell more TVs | *So the public gets fair and honest news | Because the leader asks them to | To keep the government in charge
